"Cross-references the same task or component set against itself to expose dependencies, coupled groups and possible iteration.\n\nUse when: Analyze dense task interactions that would create a tangled network. Identify work clusters that need close coordination.\n\nAvoid when: Readers need a simple chronological narrative with only a few dependencies.\n\nVisual grammar: Identical ordered labels appear on rows and columns. Off-diagonal cells indicate a defined directional dependency. Blocks and ordering reveal coupled groups under a stated convention.\n\nBuild: Choose one dependency meaning and a consistent item granularity. Record the relationships and declare whether rows depend on columns or conversely. Reorder items to reveal clusters while preserving every actual dependency.\n\nCheck: Row and column identities match exactly. Direction follows the declared convention throughout. Reordering changes presentation without changing dependency meaning.\n\nPitfalls: Mixing information, ownership and resource relationships in one unlabeled cell. Interpreting a dense block as automatically removable waste.\n\nExample: An exhibition project maps dependencies among content, lighting, fabrication and accessibility reviews, revealing a coupled design group.\n\nAlternatives: Activity network, RACI matrix."
